WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:04.863
A trust boundary is a point where data
from a less trusted or less controlled source

2
00:00:04.943 --> 00:00:06.587
enters code that may rely on it.

3
00:00:06.667 --> 00:00:09.196
In C projects, those boundaries can be
obvious,

4
00:00:09.276 --> 00:00:12.753
such as network messages or user input,
but they can also be quiet:

5
00:00:12.833 --> 00:00:16.753
configuration files, environment
variables, command-line arguments,

6
00:00:16.833 --> 00:00:21.133
IPC messages, device data, library
callbacks, and stored records loaded later.

7
00:00:21.213 --> 00:00:23.615
Treat external data as uncertain by
default.

8
00:00:23.695 --> 00:00:25.972
That does not mean every source is
hostile.

9
00:00:26.052 --> 00:00:30.225
It means the program should not assume the
source has the same invariants as the

10
00:00:30.305 --> 00:00:31.190
consuming code.

11
00:00:31.270 --> 00:00:35.569
Files can be old, incomplete, manually
edited, or generated by another version.

12
00:00:35.649 --> 00:00:38.431
Environment variables can be missing or
oversized.

13
00:00:38.511 --> 00:00:42.811
Stored data can outlive the validation
rules that were current when it was saved.

14
00:00:42.891 --> 00:00:47.190
Callbacks and integrations can introduce
trust boundaries inside the program.

15
00:00:47.270 --> 00:00:51.443
A library may call application code with
data that came from a file, a protocol,

16
00:00:51.523 --> 00:00:52.661
a plugin, or a device.

17
00:00:52.741 --> 00:00:57.294
The receiving function should know whether
the library already parsed and validated

18
00:00:57.374 --> 00:01:00.535
the data, and which checks still belong to
the application.

19
00:01:00.615 --> 00:01:02.259
Trust also depends on context.

20
00:01:02.339 --> 00:01:06.196
A value acceptable as a display label may
not be acceptable as a filename,

21
00:01:06.276 --> 00:01:10.449
array size, command argument,
configuration key, or authorization subject.

22
00:01:10.529 --> 00:01:14.512
Validation should be tied to the operation
the program is about to perform,

23
00:01:14.592 --> 00:01:17.248
not only to the source where the value
originated.

24
00:01:17.328 --> 00:01:21.880
Keeping validation close to the boundary
helps prevent uncertainty from spreading.

25
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:23.794
Once data is parsed and validated,

26
00:01:23.874 --> 00:01:28.110
the program can pass a clearer internal
representation deeper into the system.

27
00:01:28.190 --> 00:01:32.426
That makes later code easier to review
because it can rely on explicit contracts

28
00:01:32.506 --> 00:01:34.340
instead of raw input assumptions.
